    .remove(key)               // drops the key, reverting to its default
    .clear()                   // drops the whole blob

  [38;5;153mDEFAULTS = { rememberUsername: false, lastUsername: "" }[39m. The injectable [38;5;153mstorage[39m parameter is the seam that makes this
  testable without a browser and is the same seam a server-backed store would replace later.

  [1mError handling — the part that matters most here.[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is not reliably available: Safari private mode throws
  on write, browsers with storage disabled throw on access, and quota errors are real. Any throw from the backing store
  is caught and the module falls back to an in-memory object for the rest of the page's life, so a storage failure
  degrades preferences silently instead of breaking login. Corrupt or non-object JSON in the blob is treated as empty
  rather than thrown.

  [1mConsumer wiring[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains a [38;5;153m<script src="src/preferences.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m and a [38;5;153mRemember me[39m checkbox. In
  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m: on load, if [38;5;153mrememberUsername[39m is set, prefill [38;5;153m#username[39m and check the box; on successful login, either store
  [38;5;153mlastUsername[39m + [38;5;153mrememberUsername: true[39m, or [38;5;153mremove[39m both if the box is unchecked. [1mThe password is never read from or [0m
  [1mwritten to storage[0m — only the username, and only with the box checked.

  [1mFiles touched:[0m [38;5;153msrc/preferences.js[39m (new), [38;5;153mtest/preferences.test.js[39m (new), [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and
  [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are left alone.

  [1mTesting[0m — [38;5;153mcreatePreferences()[39m takes a fake Map-backed storage, plus one that throws to cover the degradation path.
  Round-trip, defaults, removal, corrupt-JSON, and storage-unavailable cases.

